From 1de7692ae23c142178b32c5a7ff566819e6b7369 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:52:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 14/91] perf(visualizer): draw the lyric glow on the compositor
 on Android

The frame HUD off the phone says the visualizer is pinned to exactly 30 fps:
p50 = p90 = p99 = 33.3 ms with only ~10 long tasks per 30 s and a 66.5 ms worst
frame. The main thread is idle, so the frames are being lost in raster. The wall
on the same device reaches 40.9 fps without the per-word glow, which rules the
device out as the ceiling.

That is the shape of the problem utils/glowBlurQuantize.ts already documents:
with the switch off, classic re-rasterises the shadow mask of every glowing
grapheme on every frame, because framer interpolates the text-shadow radius
continuously and Chromium keys its glyph strikes on the shadow's device-space
sigma. The module's own measurements put that at ~0.9 new strikes/s on Linux,
and -23 percentage points of renderer CPU on fume once the glow is drawn as a
compositor filter whose blur is cached per layer.

So this makes the platform default Linux *or Android* instead of Linux only.
Nothing else changes: the switch keeps its storage key, stays in Lab > Graphics
and the command palette, and an explicit user choice still wins.

Visual delta, measured rather than assumed: at full glow the text-shadow and
drop-shadow forms of a two-character lyric word (49 px on the phone's 816x377
landscape viewport, screenshotted at 2x) differ by 0.06 % of pixels overall and
1.06 % in the worst 8x8 region. The transition is smoother, not rougher - framer
cannot interpolate from `none` to a two-shadow string, so the old path snapped
the halo on and off, while the drop-shadow radii do interpolate
(0/0 -> 2/4 -> 4/8 -> 6/12 -> 8/16 px).

Also renamed the toggle to match what it now does, in all three locales, in the
command-palette fallbacks and in the comments that anchor to it by name.
